# `krylov::GmresResult`

The solution of a GMRES solve with per-lane residuals and the work done.

`solution` has the shape of the right-hand side; `residual_norm`, `relative_residual` and `converged` are per lane; `iterations` is the total iteration count, `operator_evaluations` the number of times the operator was applied (one per iteration plus one per restart), and `restarts` the number of restart cycles.

## Fields

### `solution`

`pluss::Series`, default `undefined`.

The solution, shaped like the right-hand side (real or complex like the input).


### `residual_norm`

`pluss::Series`, default `undefined`.

The final `‖b − A x‖` per lane.


### `relative_residual`

`pluss::Series`, default `undefined`.

The residual over `‖b‖` per lane, to compare with `relative_tolerance`.


### `converged`

`pluss::Series`, default `undefined`.

Boolean per lane: the residual met the threshold at the end of some cycle.


### `iterations`

`std::int64_t`, default `0`.

The total across restart cycles; a multiple of `restart` unless `max_iterations` cut the last cycle short.


### `operator_evaluations`

`std::int64_t`, default `0`.

How many times `A` was applied: one per iteration plus one per cycle to form the true residual. This is the cost to compare against other solvers.


### `restarts`

`std::int64_t`, default `0`.

The number of restart cycles run. A value above 1 means the Krylov basis was discarded at least once, and convergence may be slower than the unrestarted method would give.

## Guidance

Returned by `gmres`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Reading a result.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  const Series A = pluss::make_series({3.0, 1.0, -1.0, 2.0}, {2, 2});
  const auto apply = [&](const Series &x) { return A.matmul(x.unsqueeze(-1)).squeeze(-1); };
  const krylov::GmresResult r = krylov::gmres(apply, pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0}), {-1});
  out.writeln("solution =", r.solution.items<double>(), ", residual =", r.residual_norm.reshape({r.residual_norm.numel()}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("iterations =", r.iterations, ", operator evaluations =", r.operator_evaluations, ", restarts =", r.restarts, ", converged =", r.converged.reshape({r.converged.numel()}).items<bool>());
}
```

### Example 2

A batch. Each lane has its own residual and flag.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(12));
  const Series A = pluss::make_series({3.0, 1.0, -1.0, 2.0}, {2, 2});
  const auto apply = [&](const Series &x) { return A.matmul(x.unsqueeze(-1)).squeeze(-1); };
  const krylov::GmresResult r = krylov::gmres(apply, pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 0.0, 1.0, 4.0, -1.0}, {3, 2}), {-1});
  out.writeln("shape =", r.solution.sizes(), ", relative residuals =", r.relative_residual.reshape({r.relative_residual.numel()}).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

The residual check. The reported norm equals `‖b − A x‖` recomputed.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(12));
  const Series A = pluss::make_series({3.0, 1.0, -1.0, 2.0}, {2, 2});
  const auto apply = [&](const Series &x) { return A.matmul(x.unsqueeze(-1)).squeeze(-1); };
  const Series b = pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0});
  const krylov::GmresResult r = krylov::gmres(apply, b, {-1});
  out.writeln("reported =", r.residual_norm.reshape({r.residual_norm.numel()}).items<double>(), ", recomputed =", (b - apply(r.solution)).square().sum().sqrt().item<double>());
}
```

